<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert F. Stockton</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1795">1795</a>–<a href="/years/1866">1866</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert F. Stockton was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> naval officer and politician. In 1821, commanding the schooner USS Alligator, he helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-colonization-society">American Colonization Society</a>'s agent Eli Ayres obtain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-mesurado">Cape Mesurado</a> from local rulers for the future colony of Liberia. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> he led the American conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> in 1846, and he later served as a senator for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-f-stockton.jpg" alt="Robert F. Stockton" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mathew Benjamin Brady · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hon._Robert_F._Stockton,_N.J_-_NARA_-_526010_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
